-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `County Court: the party's questions`

§§ `Bringing the claim in the County Court`

@export Can the County Court of Victoria hear my civil claim (County Court Act 1958 ss 36, 37)? There is no upper money limit: the Court hears claims of any size, including personal injury and workers' compensation common law damages claims.
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of civil proceeding` @desc What the proceeding is: a claim of any other kind (most claims for injury, loss or money); a claim against a municipal council for an accident on a road, street, bridge, ferry, jetty, path, land or building it controls; a proceeding another Act gives the County Court jurisdiction over; an application for a prerogative writ (or an order in the nature of one); or a proceeding brought upon a judgment of the Supreme Court
      `excluded by another Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act excludes this kind of claim from the County Court (for example by giving it only to another court or tribunal); answer no if unsure
      `where it arose` IS A `Where the cause of action arose` @desc Where the events your claim rests on happened: wholly within Victoria; partly outside Victoria with a material part within Victoria; partly outside Victoria with no material part within Victoria; or wholly outside Victoria
      `defendant resided in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing lived in Victoria when the claim was served on them
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the County Court hear my claim` MEANS
    `both of` (`s 37 -- the County Court has jurisdiction over the kind of proceeding` `kind` `excluded by another Act`) `and also` (`s 36 -- where the cause of action arose is no bar` `where it arose` `defendant resided in Victoria`)

@export What may the County Court do with my proceeding if it is outside the Court's jurisdiction (County Court Act 1958 ss 39(2), 55)?
GIVEN `how far` IS A `How far the proceeding is beyond jurisdiction` @desc Whether the proceeding is within the Court's jurisdiction, partly beyond it, or wholly beyond it
      `consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties consent to the County Court having jurisdiction
GIVETH A `What becomes of a proceeding beyond jurisdiction`
`What happens if my claim is outside the Court's jurisdiction` MEANS
    `ss 39(2), 55 -- what becomes of a proceeding beyond jurisdiction` `how far` `consent`

@export I am suing and the defendant has counter-claimed for something beyond the County Court's jurisdiction: can the defendant be given relief beyond it (County Court Act 1958 s 51)? The counter-claim does not stop the Court deciding your own claim.
GIVEN `beyond jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant's counter-claim involves matter beyond the County Court's jurisdiction
      `written consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties (or their lawyers) have consented in writing to the County Court hearing and deciding the counter-claim
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the counter-claim get relief beyond jurisdiction` MEANS
    `s 51 -- relief beyond jurisdiction may be given on the counter-claim` `beyond jurisdiction` `written consent`

§§ `Settling an injured child's claim, and money for a child or person under disability`

-- This group arises only where the injured person is a child or cannot manage their affairs: ask "Is the injured person a
-- child, or unable to manage their own affairs?" before showing it.

@export Can I agree, on my injured child's behalf, to accept a compensation offer without suing (County Court Act 1958 s 39A(1), (10))? The agreement then needs the County Court's approval. Not for motor car (transport accident) injuries.
GIVEN `minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injured child is under 18
      `entitled to damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is, or appears to be, entitled to recover damages for bodily injury from someone (a person, company or other body)
      `motor car` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor car (a transport accident)
      `who` IS A `Who acts for the child` @desc Who you are to the child: a parent; a guardian; a next friend; a person standing in the place of a parent; the State Trust; or someone else
      `believe reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you believe the amount offered is reasonable and adequate, given the injury and the child's chances of winning in court
      `proceeding commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court proceeding for damages for the injury has already been started by or for the child
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I settle my child's injury claim` MEANS
    `s 39A(1), (10) -- I may agree for the child to accept the offer` `minor` `entitled to damages` `motor car` `who` `believe reasonable` `proceeding commenced`

@export Is the settlement agreement I made for my injured child still without any force (County Court Act 1958 s 39A(4))? It has none until it is filed with the County Court and approved by the Court's order.
GIVEN `filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement has been filed with the registrar of the County Court
      `approved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the County Court has approved the agreement by order
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my child's settlement still without force` MEANS
    `s 39A(4) -- the agreement is still without force` `filed` `approved`

@export Can the County Court approve the settlement agreement I made for my injured child (County Court Act 1958 s 39A(2), (6))?
GIVEN `filed with affidavit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement has been filed with the registrar with your affidavit verifying the facts it rests on
      `right to act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied of your right to act for the child
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that the amount is reasonable and adequate in the circumstances
      `genuine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that the agreement is genuine
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court approve my child's settlement` MEANS
    `s 39A(2), (6) -- the Court may approve the agreement` `filed with affidavit` `right to act` `reasonable` `genuine`

@export Once the County Court has approved my child's settlement, is the person who pays released from my child's injury claim (County Court Act 1958 s 39A(8))?
GIVEN `approved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the County Court has made an order approving the agreement
      `complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or body paying has complied with that order (for example, paid the money into court as ordered)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the payer released from my child's claim` MEANS
    `s 39A(8) -- the payer is released from the child's injury claim` `approved` `complied`

@export Where must money the County Court awards to a child, or to a person who cannot manage their affairs, be paid (County Court Act 1958 ss 39B, 39C)? An empty list means neither section applies.
GIVEN `minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the money is for is under 18
      `cannot manage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is incapable, because of injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, of managing their affairs in relation to the proceeding
      `ordered otherwise 39B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that a child's money be paid out otherwise than to an administrator it specifies
      `ordered otherwise 39C` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that the money not be paid into the Supreme Court
GIVETH A LIST OF `Where money for a child or person under disability goes`
`Where does the money awarded go` MEANS
    `ss 39B, 39C -- where the money awarded goes` `minor` `cannot manage` `ordered otherwise 39B` `ordered otherwise 39C`

§§ `Mediation and arbitration`

@export Can what was said or done at the mediation be used as evidence at the hearing of my proceeding (County Court Act 1958 s 47B)?
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of mediation` @desc What the mediation was: a mediation the County Court referred the proceeding to; a judicial resolution conference; or a mediation the Court did not refer (a private one)
      `agreed in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties who attended the mediation have agreed in writing that it may be used
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the mediation be used as evidence` MEANS
    `s 47B -- evidence of the mediation may be admitted at the hearing` `kind` `agreed in writing`

@export Is the arbitration award in my proceeding final, or can it be challenged (County Court Act 1958 ss 46, 47, 47A)?
GIVEN `how` IS A `How the dispute went to arbitration` @desc How the dispute went to arbitration: the parties agreed and the Court referred it to arbitrators they named (s 46); the parties agreed in writing and the Court itself acted as arbitrator (s 47); or the Court referred it under s 47A
GIVETH A `What the arbitration award is`
`Can the arbitration award be challenged` MEANS
    `ss 46, 47, 47A -- what the arbitration award is` `how`

§§ `The hearing and the trial`

@export Can the County Court decide my civil proceeding, or a matter in it, on written submissions without a hearing in person (County Court Act 1958 s 36AA)? Your consent is not needed; the Court weighs the nature of the matter, the right to a fair hearing, whether the parties could get legal advice, and whether they consent.
GIVEN `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that deciding it on the papers is in the interests of justice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my case be decided on the papers` MEANS
    `s 36AA -- my civil proceeding may be decided on the papers` `interests of justice`

@export Can I require my civil proceeding to be tried by a jury (County Court Act 1958 s 67(1))? A party may, where the amount claimed or the value of the subject-matter is more than $5000; notice goes to the registrar and the other party as the Rules direct. In a claim for unliquidated damages (such as personal injury) the judge may also require a jury.
GIVEN `amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount claimed in the proceeding, or the value of its subject-matter, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I require a jury` MEANS
    `s 67(1) -- I may require a jury` `amount claimed`

@export Can I be made to pay the jury fees (County Court Act 1958 s 68(2))? Neither party pays them where the judge required a jury of the judge's own motion.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who required the jury` @desc Who required the jury: you; the other party; the judge, of the judge's own motion; or the judge, on a party's application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be made to pay the jury fees` MEANS
    `s 68(2) -- I may be liable for the jury fees` `who`

@export Must judgment be entered in accordance with the jury's verdict in my proceeding (County Court Act 1958 s 70)? Subject to appeal.
GIVEN `verdict given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jury has given its verdict
      `leave before verdict` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge, before the verdict, gave a party leave to apply for judgment other than in accordance with the verdict
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must judgment follow the verdict` MEANS
    `s 70 -- judgment must follow the jury's verdict` `verdict given` `leave before verdict`

@export Can the County Court itself order a new trial of my proceeding (County Court Act 1958 s 73(3), (3A))? Not after a jury's verdict; otherwise the Court may, on terms, as often as it thinks fit.
GIVEN `jury verdict` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was a jury's verdict
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court order a new trial` MEANS
    `s 73(3), (3A) -- the County Court may order a new trial` `jury verdict`

§§ `Judgment and interest`

@export If the verdict or judgment is for more than I claimed, how much can I recover (County Court Act 1958 s 37(3))? The full amount, reduced only for your own contributory negligence under Part V of the Wrongs Act 1958, even above what you claimed.
GIVEN `verdict` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the verdict or judgment, in dollars, before any reduction for contributory negligence
      `amount sought` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you sought to recover in the proceeding, in dollars
      `reduction percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the Court reduced your damages for your own contributory negligence (0 if none), e.g. 25 for 25%
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Can I recover more than I claimed` MEANS
    `s 37(3) -- what I may recover above the amount I sought` `verdict` `amount sought` `reduction percent`

@export How much interest has my County Court judgment debt carried (County Court Act 1958 s 73(4))? Simple interest at the penalty interest rate fixed under the Penalty Interest Rates Act 1983, by the day over a 365-day year.
GIVEN `judgment debt` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the judgment debt (or of the costs as assessed), in dollars
      `rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The penalty interest rate fixed under section 2 of the Penalty Interest Rates Act 1983, in percent a year (e.g. 10 for 10%)
      `from` IS A DATE @desc The day interest runs from: the day judgment was entered or the order made; for costs to be assessed, the day they were assessed; or another day the Court ordered
      `to` IS A DATE @desc The day to which you want the interest worked out (e.g. the day of payment)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much interest does my judgment carry` MEANS
    `s 73(4) -- the interest on the judgment debt` `judgment debt` `rate` `from` `to`

@export Can I get a certificate of my County Court judgment, to enforce it in the Supreme Court (County Court Act 1958 s 86)? After it issues, nothing more can be done in the County Court; the Supreme Court enters final judgment with interest at the penalty rate.
GIVEN `judgment entered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether judgment has been entered in your favour (you are the judgment creditor)
      `execution barred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a period during which execution could not be issued out of the County Court is still running (for example a stay)
      `warrant against person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a warrant of execution has been issued out of the County Court under the judgment against the debtor's person
      `goods warrant outstanding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a warrant of execution against the debtor's goods has been issued out of the County Court and has not yet been returned
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I take my judgment to the Supreme Court` MEANS
    `s 86 -- I may have a certificate to take the judgment to the Supreme Court` `judgment entered` `execution barred` `warrant against person` `goods warrant outstanding`

@export My earnings are attached to pay a County Court judgment: can my employer be dealt with by the Court for dismissing or prejudicing me because of it (County Court Act 1958 s 49A(3))?
GIVEN `attachment order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order attaching your salary, wages or pension
      `prejudiced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer dismissed you, injured you in your employment, or changed your position to your disadvantage
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer did so because of the attachment order, or because you must make payments under it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my employer be dealt with for prejudicing me` MEANS
    `s 49A(3) -- my employer may be dealt with as for disobeying an order` `attachment order` `prejudiced` `because of it`

§§ `Appeals and re-hearing`

-- This group arises only after a judgment or order the person wants to challenge.

@export Where can I appeal against a County Court judgment or order in my civil proceeding, and is leave needed (County Court Act 1958 ss 17V, 47, 74, 75)? No prerogative writ lies (s 77); an application for leave to appeal to the Court of Appeal follows the Supreme Court's rules.
GIVEN `who decided` IS A `Who decided` @desc Who made the decision: a judge; an associate judge; a judicial registrar; or the Court acting as arbitrator by the parties' written agreement (s 47)
      `agreed final` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before the decision was given, the parties agreed in writing (signed by them or their lawyers) that it would be final
      `only value or place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your only ground is the Court's decision on the value of property for deciding its jurisdiction, or that the case should have been heard at another place of sitting
      `Rules provide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a judicial registrar's decision: whether the County Court Rules provide for an appeal from, or review of, it (answer yes if unsure)
GIVETH A `Where an appeal goes`
`Where can I appeal` MEANS
    `ss 17V, 47, 74, 75 -- where my appeal goes` `who decided` `agreed final` `only value or place` `Rules provide`

@export After an appeal, a re-hearing of my case before a Supreme Court judge has been directed: am I entitled to have it heard with a jury of six (County Court Act 1958 s 72(1))?
GIVEN `re-hearing directed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on an appeal to the Court of Appeal, a re-hearing before the Supreme Court constituted by a judge has been directed
      `tried with jury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the case was tried before a jury in the County Court
      `Court of Appeal ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court of Appeal ordered a jury when it directed the re-hearing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to a jury on the re-hearing` MEANS
    `s 72(1) -- I am entitled to a jury on the re-hearing` `re-hearing directed` `tried with jury` `Court of Appeal ordered`

@export Where do I stand on having a jury at the Supreme Court re-hearing: is my jury notice in time (County Court Act 1958 s 72)? The plaintiff gives it with the notice of trial; the defendant within four days after receiving notice of trial; both must pay the jury fees.
GIVEN `re-hearing directed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on an appeal to the Court of Appeal, a re-hearing before the Supreme Court constituted by a judge has been directed
      `tried with jury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the case was tried before a jury in the County Court
      `Court of Appeal ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court of Appeal ordered a jury when it directed the re-hearing
      `side` IS A `My side` @desc Whether you are the plaintiff or on the defendant side
      `notice of trial given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether notice of trial of the re-hearing has been given (by you, if you are the plaintiff) or received (by you, if you are the defendant)
      `notice of trial day` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice of trial was given (plaintiff) or received (defendant); any day if it has not been
      `jury notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the prothonotary and the other side written notice that you require a jury
      `jury notice day` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave that notice; any day if you have not
      `fees paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the proper jury fees
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where my jury on the re-hearing stands`
`Is my jury notice for the re-hearing in time` MEANS
    `s 72 -- where my jury on the re-hearing stands` (`s 72(1) -- I am entitled to a jury on the re-hearing` `re-hearing directed` `tried with jury` `Court of Appeal ordered`) `side` `notice of trial given` `notice of trial day` `jury notice given` `jury notice day` `fees paid` `today`

§§ `Costs and fees`

@export Can my lawyer's costs be allowed as costs in the cause when I recovered only a very small amount (County Court Act 1958 s 33)? Not where $20 or less is recovered, unless the Court allows them.
GIVEN `amount recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount recovered in the proceeding, in dollars
      `Court allows` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has allowed the costs of employing lawyers anyway
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my lawyer's costs be allowed on a small recovery` MEANS
    `s 33 -- my lawyer's costs may be allowed as costs in the cause` `amount recovered` `Court allows`

@export Can the County Court order my lawyer to repay unreasonable charges for my proceeding (County Court Act 1958 s 34)? You apply to the Court, which summonses the lawyer and assesses the charges.
GIVEN `employed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you employed the lawyer in the proceeding
      `unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is of the opinion that the charges or fees are unreasonable
      `costs agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is an enforceable costs agreement under the Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) between you and the lawyer that says otherwise
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my lawyer be ordered to repay charges` MEANS
    `s 34 -- the Court may order my lawyer to repay charges` `employed` `unreasonable` `costs agreement`

@export Can the County Court registrar waive my court fee because paying it would cause me financial hardship (County Court Act 1958 s 79A(6))? The registrar looks at your income, day-to-day living expenses, liabilities and assets; a waived fee is not payable.
GIVEN `hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the registrar is of the opinion that paying the fee would cause you financial hardship
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my court fee be waived` MEANS
    `s 79A(6) -- the registrar may waive my court fee` `hardship`
